## Personal setup rules (Morgan's pack)

These extend BestPractice's own conventions above; they are this repo's
personal layer (`process/personal/`), not upstream BestPractice, and they
never get exported to the public BestPractice repo.

- **On conflict with BestPractice, this pack wins.** BestPractice sets the
  default; where a rule here and a rule of BestPractice's own genuinely
  disagree on the same point, this pack's rule governs. Where this pack is
  silent, BestPractice's own rule stands undisturbed — this pack only
  narrows or overrides where it actually speaks
  ([process/personal/README.md](process/personal/README.md) §1).

- **Don't duplicate BestPractice.** This pack exists to add to BestPractice
  or override it, not restate it. A bullet here that only repeats what
  BestPractice already establishes on its own gets dropped the next time
  this file is touched, rather than left as a second copy that can drift
  out of sync with the first
  ([process/personal/README.md](process/personal/README.md) §2).

- **Commits are always authored "Morgan F".** Use Morgan's own email
  address:
  `git config user.name "Morgan F"` and
  `git config user.email "morgan@westegg.com"`
  once per clone ([tools/bootstrap.sh](tools/bootstrap.sh) does this
  automatically). This
  replaces, rather than defers to, BestPractice's own "ask before the first
  commit" default — the identity is already decided, so don't ask. It
  replaces only the *identity* half: this pack says nothing about
  co-authorship, so BestPractice's own `Co-Authored-By:` trailer naming the
  assistant still applies (§1 — where this pack is silent, BestPractice
  stands).

- **Every date is a Buenos Aires local calendar date**, not the session's
  system clock. Two places this bites:
  - Any date written into a document ("as of / verified `<date>`" per
    BestPractice practice 16, a file's last-updated header below) uses
    `America/Argentina/Buenos_Aires` local time.
  - Git commit timestamps: prefix the commit command with
    `TZ="America/Argentina/Buenos_Aires"` so the recorded author/committer
    offset is Buenos Aires', not the container's —
    `TZ="America/Argentina/Buenos_Aires" git commit -m "..."`.
  (Argentina has held UTC−3 year-round, no daylight saving, since 2009 —
  verified 2026-08-21; re-verify if this rule is ever touched and the fact
  seems dated.)

- **Files carry a "last updated" timestamp, who made it, and a version
  number — near the top, when reasonable.** For a Markdown file: `<!--
  Last updated: YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS (Buenos Aires) by NAME, to version N
  -->` as the first line — a full timestamp, hour/minute/second, not just
  a calendar date; a bare date can't tell two same-day edits apart. `NAME`
  is whoever made the edit — always "Morgan F" here, the same name every
  commit is already authored as, so the header shows who last touched the
  file without a `git blame`. `N` is a plain integer counter private to
  that one file: 1 the first time the header is added, +1 each subsequent
  time the file's content changes. The "to version N" phrasing is
  deliberate — it names that file's own version, not a repo-wide release
  number, so bumping it is a one-file edit. That's also why installing
  this rule doesn't mean adding the header to every existing file at once:
  a file only picks up (or bumps) its header when it's already being
  edited for some other reason — **only the files actually changed**, never
  a repo-wide sweep. For a script or config format with comments, the
  equivalent in that syntax. Skip it where a leading comment would break
  the file (JSON has none) or where the file is a vendored, byte-for-byte
  copy under `process/upstream/` — that tree must track upstream exactly,
  never a local edit. Update the line whenever you touch the file's
  content, not its formatting alone — same trigger for bumping `N`.

- **Commit messages link the assistant session where the change was
  planned.** Add a trailer, `Session: <url>` — for Claude Code,
  `https://claude.ai/code/session_<ID>` (the running session's own ID; ask
  the harness for it if it isn't already visible). For an unattended run
  with no chat session behind it (the sync workflows below), use that run's
  own URL instead. If a tool genuinely has no shareable link, say so in the
  trailer (`Session: none available (<tool>)`) rather than dropping it
  silently — the point is a reviewer can always tell whether a link was
  considered and skipped, or just forgotten.

- **Decide small calls yourself; only stop for big ones.** Default to
  continuing, not asking. When a judgment call is needed to keep moving and
  it's small or moderate — a default value, a phrasing choice, picking
  between two reasonable implementations, an ambiguity that doesn't change
  the shape of what gets delivered — make the call and note it in **both**
  the normal end-of-work reply that already lists files touched, *and* the
  commit message itself, under a "Judgment calls made:" heading (the same
  pattern the sync workflows already use for their own "Judgment calls to
  review:" heading — see the sync bullets below). The chat reply is easy to
  lose once a thread scrolls on; the commit message is what survives into
  `git log` and the PR diff, so it stays visible to someone reviewing the
  merged PR later, not just to someone reading the reply in the moment.
  Skip the heading on a commit that made no judgment calls — don't pad every commit with
  "none" — but never leave a real one out of the commit message. Reserve
  stopping and asking for calls that are genuinely big: hard or costly to
  undo, change what gets delivered or to whom, spend real money, touch
  credentials or production, or are a toss-up two reasonable people would
  clearly land differently on.

- **`go` or `merge` as a standalone final sentence means: commit and merge
  to main.** If Morgan's message, at a point where you've said you're
  ready, ends with `go` or `merge` as its own sentence — on its own line,
  or set off by a preceding sentence-ending punctuation mark,
  case-insensitive — that's authorization, right there, to commit the
  pending work and merge it into the default branch, using this repo's
  usual conventions (identity, timestamp, session trailer, gates), without
  asking again first. "That was perfect. merge" and "Go." both count;
  "let's merge the two lists" and "go check the logs" don't — the word has
  to stand alone as the message's last sentence, not appear as part of a
  longer one. If it's ambiguous whether the trailing word is this shorthand
  or ordinary language in context, ask rather than assume. This is
  shorthand for the same authorization the "merge only when the user says
  so" default above already asks for
  ([process/personal/README.md](process/personal/README.md) §8).

- **TODO.md gate — step 0c of the merge runbook, before every push.**
  Immediately after the export gate (0b): re-read this thread's discussion
  against [TODO.md](TODO.md). Add any idea that came up but never got a
  line; remove or check off anything this branch has now implemented.
  `TODO.md` drifting out of sync with what was actually decided is the
  specific failure this gate exists to catch — do it every push, not just
  when something feels stale.

- **Agent-relevant instructions in a README or other key file also go in
  `AGENTS.md`.** A README, `CONTRIBUTING.md`,
  [GETTING_STARTED.md](GETTING_STARTED.md), or any other key file can pick up its own operational instructions over
  time — a setup step, a gotcha, a rule about how to work in the repo —
  written where a human reader would look, not where an agent is told to
  look. When such a file gains an instruction useful for an agent to know
  (the operational kind: how to build it, a constraint on how to work, a
  step an agent would otherwise miss — not general project description or
  end-user documentation), fold the same instruction into `AGENTS.md` too,
  in its own words if that reads better there. This runs both directions:
  whether the instruction lands first in the other file and needs pulling
  into `AGENTS.md`, or is written into `AGENTS.md` directly and never
  mirrored back to where a human would expect to read it, either way the
  same instruction ends up in both places. It doesn't run the other way for
  content that only belongs in one place — a README's marketing framing has
  no business in `AGENTS.md`, and `AGENTS.md`'s own meta-structure has no
  business padding out a README. Check for this at the capture gate (merge
  runbook step 0) the same way any other captured decision gets folded in
  ([process/personal/README.md](process/personal/README.md) §10).

- **A light check runs on every commit path**
  ([.github/workflows/light-check.yml](.github/workflows/light-check.yml),
  [process/personal/tools/light_check.py](process/personal/tools/light_check.py))
  — conflict markers, invalid JSON/YAML/Python syntax, secret-shaped
  strings, broken doc links, and (repo-wide, every run) that
  `process/personal/` is actually vendored: `process/manifest_personal.json`
  exists, parses, has entries, and every entry's file exists on disk —
  catches the pack having been copied in rather than installed per
  [process/personal/README.md](process/personal/README.md) §18. Run it
  yourself before committing, the same way you already run
  [doc_lint.py](process/upstream/tools/doc_lint.py) and
  [practice_audit.py](process/upstream/tools/practice_audit.py); CI runs it
  too, so it binds even if a session forgets.

- **LLM integrations stay platform-neutral; OpenRouter is the default
  assumption.** Any system this pack helps set up that talks to an LLM
  should be built against a provider-neutral interface — model name, token,
  and base URL as swappable config, not hard-wired to one vendor's SDK or
  auth shape — so switching providers, or using whichever token is on hand,
  is a config change, not a rewrite. Absent other instruction, assume the
  credential in hand is an OpenRouter token, not a given vendor's own key;
  OpenRouter's API is itself OpenAI-request-shaped *(verified 2026-08-22)*,
  so it's also a sensible default shape for the neutral interface itself
  ([process/personal/README.md](process/personal/README.md) §12).

- **Always fail gracefully.** Any code you write or set up here anticipates
  its own common failure modes rather than letting them surface as an
  unhandled crash. Before shipping code that depends on something outside
  its own control — a required config or environment variable, a file
  that's supposed to exist, a network call, a third-party credential —
  check for the absence or failure case on purpose and degrade in a way
  the caller can act on: a clear error message naming exactly what's
  missing and how to fix it, a documented fallback/default, or a clean
  non-zero exit — never a raw stack trace, a silent wrong answer, or a
  hang ([process/personal/README.md](process/personal/README.md) §13).

- **A scheduled check keeps BestPractice itself current — weekly by
  default, one line to switch to daily**
  ([.github/workflows/bestpractice-upstream-sync.yml](.github/workflows/bestpractice-upstream-sync.yml),
  from
  [process/personal/templates/github-actions/upstream-sync.yml.template](process/personal/templates/github-actions/upstream-sync.yml.template)):
  unattended, it takes any upstream update, integrates it with its own
  judgment, and merges it — recording every judgment call in the commit
  message. A session that notices the sync's PR merged with judgment
  calls listed should skim them like any other change; they are flagged
  precisely because they're the ones worth a second look. Authenticates
  with either `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN` (a Pro/Max subscriber's token from
  `claude setup-token`) or `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` — only one is required.

- **A second scheduled check keeps the personal pack itself current —
  same weekly-default, daily-optional cadence**
  (`.github/workflows/personal-pack-sync.yml`, from
  [process/personal/templates/github-actions/personal-pack-sync.yml.template](process/personal/templates/github-actions/personal-pack-sync.yml.template)):
  same shape as the BestPractice sync above, but for `process/personal/`
  against its own source, RepoPersonalPreferences (private —
  [process/manifest_personal.json](process/manifest_personal.json)'s
  `upstream.repo`/`upstream.commit` track it the same way
  [process/manifest.json](process/manifest.json) tracks BestPractice). It
  needs its own repository secret, `PERSONAL_PACK_TOKEN`, since that source
  repo is private — see
  [process/personal/README.md](process/personal/README.md) §15. That
  secret can't be set by any session; every run of the workflow's `check`
  job warns (a `::warning::` Actions annotation) if the secret is missing —
  a per-run smoke test, not just a one-time install reminder — but if it's
  ever missing, say so plainly in your own reply too rather than assuming
  it's handled.

- **Both scheduled syncs skip cleanly, not loudly, when neither Claude
  credential is set.** Both check whether `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN` or
  `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` is set before attempting the update; if their source
  moved but both are blank, the `update` job is skipped and a
  `::warning::` lands in that run's summary instead of an auth failure.
  **If asked to run one of these syncs manually** (or you notice
  mid-thread that one is overdue and the user wants it done now), you
  don't need either secret at all — you're already Claude — so just take
  the update yourself, in-session, following the same steps the workflow
  would, then remind the user once, plainly, to set
  `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN` or `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` so future runs don't
  need to be asked for by hand.

- **A session-start notice asks about drift immediately, not at the
  end.** `tools/bootstrap.sh` runs both freshness notices on every session
  start now, no model call needed: `checkin.py fresh` (already wired,
  BestPractice's own, practice 13) and `pack_sync.py fresh`
  ([process/personal/tools/pack_sync.py](process/personal/tools/pack_sync.py),
  wired per
  [process/personal/README.md](process/personal/README.md) §18 step 4,
  from
  [process/personal/templates/bootstrap-freshness.sh.template](process/personal/templates/bootstrap-freshness.sh.template)).
  Each prints a one-line notice only when its source has moved —
  `process/manifest.json`'s recorded BestPractice commit against
  BestPractice's actual HEAD, or `process/manifest_personal.json`'s
  recorded commit against RepoPersonalPreferences's actual HEAD — silent
  otherwise, never a gate. Because this runs as part of the same
  session-start hook that already sets up the environment, raise whichever
  notice fired as part of catching the member up, not something saved for
  the end. Taking either update stays deliberate whenever it's raised —
  never without being asked, since that's the one deliberate difference
  from the scheduled syncs above, which merge unattended. If asked to
  proceed, take the update the same way a manually-requested sync already
  does, opened as a PR the user approves and merges themselves rather than
  auto-merged. This doesn't replace either scheduled sync — they still
  bound the gap between sessions when nobody opens the repo; this only
  closes the narrower gap where a session (and a person to ask) is already
  here. **Fallback, for whenever a session-start notice didn't fire or
  drift happened mid-session:** repeat the same cheap `git ls-remote`
  comparisons at the end of the session too, right after the merge
  runbook's own steps. **This also has to survive an indirect install:**
  the comparisons above assume the repo is fully installed, which isn't
  guaranteed when either tree arrived some way other than a session
  running the install procedure — a fork, a template-repo copy, or a repo
  vendored from an *already-dependent* repo rather than from canonical
  source. So this same check also verifies, whenever `process/upstream/`
  and/or `process/personal/` is present: does
  `.github/workflows/bestpractice-upstream-sync.yml` exist, does
  `.github/workflows/personal-pack-sync.yml` exist, does
  `tools/bootstrap.sh` actually carry both freshness snippets, and does
  this repo's own `AGENTS.md` actually carry these woven-in sections? If
  something's missing, that's an incomplete install, not drift — say so
  plainly and offer to finish it now, sourced from the canonical repo (the
  public BestPractice repo; RepoPersonalPreferences for the pack),
  regardless of which repo the files on disk actually arrived from
  ([process/personal/README.md](process/personal/README.md) §16).

- **This repo's default branch is `main`, checked once at install.**
  GitHub only defaults a freshly-created repo to `main` on its own — a
  repo that predates that default, or arrived some other way (an import,
  a mirror, an org policy), can still sit on `master` or something else.
  If this repo's default branch isn't already `main`, set it once — via
  the GitHub API's repo-update endpoint where the session's tools reach
  that far, otherwise as a one-click item (Settings → General → Default
  branch) disclosed in [GETTING_STARTED.md](GETTING_STARTED.md)'s
  administrator section like any other click-path
  ([process/personal/README.md](process/personal/README.md) §17). One-time
  only — no need to recheck it once set.
